The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions on Haiti, in particular resolutions 2476 (2019), 2653 (2022), 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023), 2743 (2024), 2751 (2024), 2752 (2024) and 2785 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, unity and prosperity of Haiti, and to the ownership by the Haitian people of the political process in their country,

Reaffirming the primary responsibility of the Government of Haiti to protect and respect human rights and to ensure security and stability, with the support of the international community, and recalling the need for all actors to comply with international humanitarian law and human rights law, as applicable,

Expressing grave concern at the continued deterioration of the security and humanitarian situation in Haiti, the expansion of gang control over Port-au-Prince and other areas, the rise in killings, sexual and gender-based violence, child recruitment, displacement and hunger, and the severe impact on the population, including children and vulnerable groups,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks by armed gangs and criminal networks, including the attacks in Port-au-Prince, Cité Soleil, Pont-Sondé and Kenscoff, the use of sexual violence and explosive devices, and attacks against the Haitian National Police, medical personnel, humanitarian workers, journalists and civilian infrastructure,

Deeply concerned by the illicit trafficking of arms and ammunition into Haiti, which continues to fuel gang violence, and stressing the importance of full implementation of the sanctions measures imposed by resolutions 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023) and 2752 (2024), and of international and regional cooperation to disrupt trafficking networks,

Welcoming the continued efforts of the Haitian National Police and the Multinational Security Support Mission, and the contributions of Member States and regional organizations to the Mission, while urging greater and sustained voluntary contributions to address funding, equipment and logistical shortfalls,

Recognizing the role of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH), as extended by resolution 2785 (2025), in supporting a Haitian-led and Haitian-owned political process, good offices, electoral support and the strengthening of institutions, and emphasizing the need for close coordination among BINUH, the Haitian authorities, the Multinational Security Support Mission and regional organizations, including the Caribbean Community and the Organization of American States,

Reaffirming its support for a credible, inclusive and participatory political transition in Haiti, leading to the holding of free and fair legislative and presidential elections and the seating of a democratically elected president by February 2026, and stressing the need to protect the security and participation of all candidates, voters, observers and civil society, including women and youth,

Recalling the Secretary-General’s recommendations of 24 February 2025 on options for the possible role of the United Nations in sustaining security and stability in Haiti, and underscoring the need for contingency planning for a possible transition to a United Nations peacekeeping operation, consistent with conditions on the ground, the consent of the Government of Haiti and the availability of resources,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the authorization of the Multinational Security Support Mission (MSS), as set out in resolution 2699 (2023) and extended by resolution 2751 (2024), until 2 October 2026;

2. Decides that the MSS is authorized, in close cooperation and coordination with the Government of Haiti and the Haitian National Police, and within the limits of its capabilities and areas of deployment, to take all necessary measures to:
(a) support the Haitian National Police in efforts to protect civilians and restore public order and security;
(b) protect critical infrastructure, including ports, airports, telecommunications, hospitals, schools, humanitarian corridors and sites of religious and cultural significance;
(c) support the safe and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance and the protection of humanitarian personnel;
(d) support the disarmament, demobilization and reintegration of gang members, in coordination with the Haitian authorities and relevant United Nations agencies, and the surrender and safe storage of weapons;
(e) support the Haitian authorities in planning and preparing for secure elections, including the protection of electoral infrastructure, personnel and voters, at the request of and in coordination with the Government of Haiti;
(f) contribute to the protection of women and children from violence, including sexual and gender-based violence, and to the promotion of human rights and accountability, including through appropriate training and civilian oversight;

3. Emphasizes that the MSS shall operate under Haitian leadership, with the primary responsibility of the Government of Haiti, and in full respect of Haiti’s sovereignty, and that all operations and conduct shall be consistent with international law, including human rights law and international humanitarian law, and with the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy, and requests the MSS to establish robust mechanisms for civilian harm mitigation, investigations of allegations of misconduct and accountability;

4. Reaffirms the measures imposed by resolutions 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023) and 2752 (2024), and calls upon all Member States to implement fully the arms embargo, asset freeze and travel ban, and to take steps to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer of arms and related materiel to designated individuals and entities, and further calls for enhanced international cooperation to combat illicit arms trafficking, including through border security and information-sharing;

5. Calls upon Member States, regional organizations and international partners to provide voluntary contributions, equipment, training, logistical support and personnel to the MSS and to the Haitian National Police, in response to the Secretary-General’s appeals and the needs identified by the Government of Haiti, and encourages the Secretary-General to continue to coordinate international support, including through a trust fund and transparent reporting;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide the MSS with logistical, technical and administrative support, within existing resources and in coordination with the Haitian authorities, consistent with resolution 2699 (2023), and to ensure effective coordination between the United Nations, BINUH, the MSS and the Haitian National Police;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to consult with the Government of Haiti, the MSS, BINUH, regional organizations and other stakeholders on options for a possible transition from the MSS to a United Nations peacekeeping operation, including timelines, benchmarks and resource requirements, building on the recommendations of 24 February 2025, and to report to the Council no later than 15 January 2026;

8. Further requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution, including the situation on the ground, progress towards the political transition and elections, the security and humanitarian situation, the implementation of the sanctions regime and allegations of human rights violations and abuses, no later than 15 January 2026 and every 90 days thereafter;

9. Expresses its intention to review the authorization of the MSS before 2 October 2026, based on the report of the Secretary-General and the situation on the ground, and to remain actively seized of the matter.

