Recognizing the role of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH), as extended by resolution 2785 (2025), in supporting a Haitian-led and Haitian-owned political process, good offices, electoral support and the strengthening of institutions, including the judicial and penal systems, and emphasizing the need for close coordination among BINUH, the Haitian authorities, the Multinational Security Support Mission and regional organizations, including the Caribbean Community and the Organization of American States,

Reaffirming its support for a credible, inclusive and participatory political transition in Haiti, leading to the holding of free and fair legislative and presidential elections and the seating of a democratically elected president by February 2026, when conditions permit, and stressing the need to protect the security and participation of all candidates, voters, observers and civil society, and to ensure the full, equal and meaningful participation of women, youth and civil society in political processes and elections,

Recalling the Secretary-General’s recommendations of 24 February 2025 on options for the possible role of the United Nations in sustaining security and stability in Haiti, and underscoring the need for contingency planning for a possible transition to a United Nations peacekeeping operation or other appropriate United Nations support arrangements, consistent with conditions on the ground, the consent of the Government of Haiti, the availability of resources and a further decision of the Security Council,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the authorization of the Multinational Security Support Mission (MSS), as set out in resolution 2699 (2023) and extended by resolution 2751 (2024), until 2 October 2026, at the request and with the ongoing consent of the Government of Haiti and in full respect of Haiti’s sovereignty and territorial integrity;

2. Decides that the MSS is authorized, at the request of and in close cooperation and coordination with the Government of Haiti and the Haitian National Police, in full respect of Haiti’s sovereignty and territorial integrity, and within the limits of its capabilities and areas of deployment, to take all necessary measures to:
(a) support the Haitian National Police in efforts to protect civilians and restore public order and security;
(b) protect critical infrastructure, including ports, airports, telecommunications, hospitals, schools, humanitarian corridors and sites of religious and cultural significance;
(c) support the safe, rapid, sustained and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance, including across conflict lines and at border crossings, and the protection of humanitarian personnel;
(d) support the disarmament, demobilization and reintegration of gang members, including children formerly associated with armed gangs and other vulnerable individuals, with appropriate child protection safeguards, ensuring that such children are treated primarily as victims and handed over to civilian child protection actors in accordance with international law, in coordination with the Haitian authorities and relevant United Nations agencies, and support the surrender and safe storage of weapons, as well as community violence reduction and reintegration support;
(e) support the Haitian authorities in planning and preparing for secure elections, including the protection of electoral infrastructure, personnel and voters, at the request of and in close coordination with the Government of Haiti, and in coordination with BINUH, and through measures to promote the safe, full, equal and meaningful participation of women, youth and civil society in electoral and political processes;
(f) contribute to the protection of women and children from violence, including sexual and gender-based violence, and to the promotion of human rights and accountability, including through appropriate pre-deployment and in-mission training on human rights, child protection and the prevention of sexual exploitation and abuse, with a zero-tolerance policy, and robust civilian oversight;
(g) support the strengthening and professionalization of the Haitian National Police, including through training, mentoring, equipment and human rights compliance, in coordination with BINUH and relevant partners;

3. Emphasizes that the MSS shall operate under Haitian leadership, with the primary responsibility of the Government of Haiti, and in full respect of Haiti’s sovereignty, and that all operations and conduct shall be consistent with international law, including human rights law and international humanitarian law, and with the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy, and requests the MSS to establish, in consultation with BINUH, OHCHR and Haitian civil society, independent, transparent and accessible mechanisms for civilian harm mitigation, prompt investigation of allegations of misconduct and accountability, including through appropriate training and civilian oversight, and to report to the Council through the Secretary-General on their operation and outcomes, while protecting victims’ confidentiality and ensuring full compliance with the Human Rights Due Diligence Policy;

4. Reaffirms the measures imposed by resolutions 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023) and 2752 (2024), and calls upon all Member States to implement fully the arms embargo, asset freeze and travel ban, and to take steps to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer of arms and related materiel to designated individuals and entities; further calls for enhanced international cooperation to combat illicit arms trafficking, including through border security, maritime interdiction, inspection of cargo suspected of carrying arms and ammunition to Haiti, information-sharing, including through regional mechanisms, and measures to prevent diversion to armed gangs, including by addressing the external sources and transfer routes of illicit weapons, with full respect for the sovereignty of States; requests the Sanctions Committee to strengthen monitoring and reporting on the arms embargo, including illicit trafficking and diversion networks, and calls on all Member States, especially neighboring States, to enhance border controls and prevent arms and ammunition from reaching armed gangs; calls on Member States to report to the Security Council and the relevant sanctions committee on measures taken to implement the arms embargo, including through border, customs and maritime cooperation; and reaffirms that the measures imposed by resolutions 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023) and 2752 (2024) do not apply to the provision of humanitarian assistance and other activities consistent with resolution 2664 (2022);

5. Calls upon Member States, regional organizations and international partners to provide predictable, sustainable and adequate voluntary contributions, equipment, training, logistical support and personnel to the MSS and to the Haitian National Police, in response to the Secretary-General’s appeals and the needs identified by the Government of Haiti, and encourages the Secretary-General to continue to coordinate international support, including through a trust fund and transparent reporting; and requests the Secretary-General, in consultation with the Government of Haiti, the MSS and troop- and police-contributing countries, to explore options for predictable, sustainable and adequate financing for the MSS and, as appropriate, the Haitian National Police, including through a United Nations trust fund and other available options, and to report to the Council by 15 January 2026;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide the MSS with logistical, technical and administrative support, within existing resources and without prejudice to the mandate and resources of BINUH, in coordination with the Haitian authorities, consistent with resolution 2699 (2023), and to ensure effective coordination between the United Nations, BINUH, the MSS and the Haitian National Police;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to include in the report requested in paragraph 9 detailed proposals for enhancing BINUH’s role in supporting the Haitian-led political transition, good offices, electoral support and coordination with the MSS to bolster enforcement action against gangs, building on the recommendations of 24 February 2025;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to consult with the Government of Haiti, troop- and police-contributing countries, the MSS, BINUH, regional organizations, including the Caribbean Community and the Organization of American States, and other stakeholders on the full range of options for the possible future role of the United Nations in sustaining security and stability in Haiti, including a possible transition from the MSS to a United Nations peacekeeping operation or other appropriate United Nations support arrangements, as well as options for a possible future international security presence, provided that any such transition or future international security presence shall be conditions-based, gradual and not automatic, require the consent of the Government of Haiti and be subject to a further decision of the Security Council, and shall include clear and measurable benchmarks, timelines, conditions, resource requirements and an exit strategy, with a phased approach and in close consultation with the Caribbean Community and other regional stakeholders, with due attention to child protection and women, peace and security expertise, building on the recommendations of 24 February 2025, and to report to the Council no later than 15 January 2026;

9. Further requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution, based on verified information and in consultation with the Government of Haiti, BINUH and Haitian civil society, including the situation on the ground, progress towards the political transition and elections, the security and humanitarian situation, including humanitarian access and displacement, with sex- and age-disaggregated data where possible, the implementation of the sanctions regime and arms-embargo violations, allegations of human rights violations and abuses, including sexual and gender-based violence and the recruitment and use of children, the implementation of the Human Rights Due Diligence Policy and civilian harm mitigation mechanisms, and allegations of misconduct by MSS personnel and the status of investigations, no later than 15 January 2026 and every 90 days thereafter;

10. Expresses its intention to review the authorization of the MSS upon the report requested in paragraph 9, due no later than 15 January 2026, and every 90 days thereafter, with a view to considering any adjustment of the mandate, size, composition or transition timeline in light of conditions on the ground, and to review the authorization before 2 October 2026, and to remain actively seized of the matter.

