4. Reaffirms the measures imposed by resolutions 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023) and 2752 (2024), and calls upon all Member States to implement fully the arms embargo, asset freeze and travel ban, and to take steps to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer of arms and related materiel to designated individuals and entities; further calls for enhanced international cooperation to combat illicit arms trafficking, including through border security, maritime interdiction, inspection of cargo suspected of carrying arms and ammunition to Haiti, information-sharing, including through regional mechanisms, and measures to prevent diversion to armed gangs, including by addressing the external sources and transfer routes of illicit weapons, with full respect for the sovereignty of States; requests the Sanctions Committee to strengthen monitoring and reporting on the arms embargo, including illicit trafficking and diversion networks, and calls on all Member States, especially neighboring States, to enhance border controls and prevent arms and ammunition from reaching armed gangs; calls on Member States to report to the Security Council and the relevant sanctions committee on measures taken to implement the arms embargo, including through border, customs and maritime cooperation; and reaffirms that the measures imposed by resolutions 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023) and 2752 (2024) do not apply to the provision of humanitarian assistance and other activities consistent with resolution 2664 (2022);

5. Calls upon Member States, regional organizations and international partners to provide predictable, sustainable and adequate voluntary contributions, equipment, training, logistical support and personnel to the MSS and to the Haitian National Police, in response to the Secretary-General’s appeals and the needs identified by the Government of Haiti, and encourages the Secretary-General to continue to coordinate international support, including through a trust fund and transparent reporting; and requests the Secretary-General, in consultation with the Government of Haiti, the MSS and troop- and police-contributing countries, to explore options for predictable, sustainable and adequate financing for the MSS and, as appropriate, the Haitian National Police, including through a United Nations trust fund, assessed contributions, as appropriate, and other available options, to address funding, equipment and logistical shortfalls, and to report to the Council by 15 January 2026;

6. Underlines that the security situation in Haiti requires a comprehensive approach addressing its root causes, and calls upon Member States and international partners to scale up humanitarian assistance and support for reconstruction, food security and socioeconomic development in Haiti;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide the MSS with logistical, technical and administrative support, within existing resources and without prejudice to the mandate and resources of BINUH, in coordination with the Haitian authorities, consistent with resolution 2699 (2023), and to ensure effective coordination between the United Nations, BINUH, the MSS and the Haitian National Police;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to include in the report requested in paragraph 10 detailed proposals for enhancing BINUH’s role in supporting the Haitian-led political transition, good offices, electoral support and coordination with the MSS to bolster enforcement action against gangs, building on the recommendations of 24 February 2025;

9. Requests the Secretary-General to consult with the Government of Haiti, troop- and police-contributing countries, the MSS, BINUH, regional organizations, including the Caribbean Community and the Organization of American States, and other stakeholders on the full range of options for the possible future role of the United Nations in sustaining security and stability in Haiti, including a possible transition from the MSS to a United Nations peacekeeping operation or other appropriate United Nations support arrangements, as well as options for a possible future international security presence, provided that any such transition or future international security presence shall be conditions-based, gradual and not automatic, require the consent of the Government of Haiti and be subject to a further decision of the Security Council, and shall include clear and measurable benchmarks, timelines, conditions, resource requirements and an exit strategy, with a phased approach and in close consultation with the Caribbean Community and other regional stakeholders, with due attention to child protection and women, peace and security expertise, building on the recommendations of 24 February 2025, and to report to the Council no later than 15 January 2026;

10. Further requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution, based on verified information and in consultation with the Government of Haiti, BINUH and Haitian civil society, including the situation on the ground, progress towards the political transition and elections, the security and humanitarian situation, including humanitarian access and displacement, with sex- and age-disaggregated data where possible, the implementation of the sanctions regime and arms-embargo violations, allegations of human rights violations and abuses, including sexual and gender-based violence and the recruitment and use of children, the implementation of the Human Rights Due Diligence Policy and civilian harm mitigation mechanisms, and allegations of misconduct by MSS personnel and the status of investigations, no later than 15 January 2026 and every 90 days thereafter;

11. Expresses its intention to review the authorization of the MSS upon the report requested in paragraph 10, due no later than 15 January 2026, and every 90 days thereafter, with a view to considering any adjustment of the mandate, size, composition or transition timeline in light of conditions on the ground, and to review the authorization before 2 October 2026, and to remain actively seized of the matter.
